# SAEQ DRIVER — Development Governance Policy
## Permanent Engineering Directive · Version 1.1

These rules are **binding** for all current and future SAEQ Driver development
work. They may be overridden only by an explicit accepted ADR (`docs/adr/`)
or an explicit decision from the project owner.

Canonical document: `docs/DEVELOPMENT_GOVERNANCE_POLICY.md`.

---

## 1. Development philosophy

Development must be: Incremental, Small scope, Testable, Reviewable,
Reversible, Documented. Large, unreviewable changes are forbidden.

---

## 2. Feature lifecycle (mandatory order)

1. Review the Roadmap.
2. Review the Decision Hierarchy (§14).
3. Create an independent Feature Branch.
4. Implement **one** feature only.
5. Run the phase tests.
6. Runtime Validation if operational behavior changed.
7. Quality Gate.
8. Exactly **one** Commit.
9. Review and approval.
10. Only then move to the next phase.

Skipping a step is forbidden.

---

## 3. Git policy

- Start from the last **approved** commit.
- Independent branch per Feature or Phase.
- No direct work on the main/default branch.
- No merging multiple primary features into one commit.
- No `git push` before phase approval.
- No starting the next phase before the current one is closed.
- Conventional Commits only.

---

## 4. Scope control

Forbidden: out-of-scope features, unrelated opportunistic fixes, large
refactors, architecture changes without ADR, new dependencies without
approval, any Customer/Merchant/Admin work in this Driver repo.

If out-of-scope work appears: **stop and report**.

---

## 5. Testing policy

Add or update appropriate Unit / Widget / Integration / Runtime tests.
Do not reduce test count or remove tests without a documented reason.

---

## 6. Quality Gate (before every commit)

`dart format` (modified files) · `git diff --check` · `flutter analyze` ·
`flutter test` · `flutter build` · Runtime Validation when behavior changed.
A successful build alone is **not** enough.

---

## 7. Runtime Validation

Phases affecting Navigation, Authentication, Startup, Lifecycle, State
Restoration, Services, Routing, or Persistence **must** be validated on a
real Android device. Emulator is not a substitute without a documented reason.

---

## 8. Technical debt (see also §17)

Out-of-scope issues: do not auto-fix, do not ignore, document as debt,
propose when to address. Fix in-phase only if blocking / security / data-loss
/ Quality Gate failure (§17).

---

## 9. Documentation

Every phase needs an official report: goal, scope, files, tests, runtime,
risks, debt, Commit Hash, decision, next-phase recommendation (§19).
Update the Roadmap when the overall plan changes.

---

## 10. Security

No real keys/tokens/secrets/hardcoded passwords. No temporary security
bypasses. No Fake implementations runnable in Production without a Guard.

---

## 11. Definition of Done

Goal met · required tests passed · Runtime done when needed · no regression ·
docs updated · exactly one Commit · no `git push` · phase reviewed/approved.

---

## 12. Mandatory stop rule

Stop and request review before: architecture change, new dependency, router
rewrite, state-management redesign, radical DB change, security-contract
change, production backend integration, deleting core files, changing an ADR,
or anything outside scope.

---

## 13. Golden principle

**Quality over speed.** Close a phase only when it is stable, testable,
documented, maintainable, and extensible.

---

# Appendix A — Decision Hierarchy and AI Recommendation Policy

## 14. Decision Hierarchy

When instructions, documents, or recommendations conflict, higher wins:

1. Architectural Decision Records (ADR)
2. PROJECT_BIBLE
3. ENTERPRISE_ARCHITECTURE
4. SYSTEM_ARCHITECTURE
5. DEVELOPMENT_GOVERNANCE_POLICY
6. PHASE ROADMAP
7. Current Phase Instructions
8. Current Task Instructions
9. AI recommendations

Do not skip this order. On conflict: adopt the higher source; document why a
lower source was set aside if it affects execution; do not invent a new
architectural decision during implementation without an ADR or explicit
approval.

Platform constraints in the Master Development Directive (Driver-only app,
no role merging, etc.) remain binding because they rest on ADR-013 and
PROJECT_BIBLE — i.e. ranks above this policy.

---

## 15. AI Recommendation Policy

Any recommendation from Cursor, ChatGPT, or another assistant is an
**engineering suggestion**, not a binding decision.

Before acting, verify alignment with: Decision Hierarchy, current
Architecture, ADR, Roadmap, current phase scope, quality and security.

If it conflicts with any higher-ranked source: reject, defer, or escalate to
a new ADR. **Do not implement it directly.**

---

## 16. Architectural Change Policy

Treat as architectural (stop, assess, ADR if needed, wait for approval):

- Layering changes
- State Management changes
- Dependency Injection changes
- Routing strategy changes
- Database changes
- Security model changes
- Authentication flow changes
- Shared services that affect all features

---

## 17. Technical Debt Policy

Document · classify severity · link to the right future phase · do not fix
out-of-scope debt unless it blocks the phase, is a security defect, risks
data loss, or blocks the Quality Gate.

---

## 18. Quality Regression Policy

Do not accept a phase that regresses quality: undocumented drop in test
count, new analyze errors, build/runtime failure, weaker security, clear
performance drop, or breakage of a previously stable feature.

Fix before close, or document and get an explicit exception.

---

## 19. Documentation Policy

No phase is closed without its official report (goal, scope, files, tests,
runtime, risks, debt, Commit Hash, decision, next-phase recommendation).

---

## 20. Long-Term Maintainability Principle

Balance maintainability, scalability, testability, code clarity, and
long-term stability. Do not sacrifice these for temporary speed.

---

## 21. Golden Rule

- Speed vs quality → **quality wins**.
- Easy implementation vs correct architecture → **keep the architecture**.
- AI suggestion vs official docs → **official docs win**.

The goal is not finishing the most phases — it is building a stable, secure,
scalable, maintainable platform for years.
